  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "MOD-GAUSSIAN CONVERGENCE AND ITS APPLICATIONS FOR MODELS OF STATISTICAL MECHANICS — In this paper we complete our understanding of the role played by the lim- iting (or residue) function in the\n\n**MOD-GAUSSIAN CONVERGENCE AND ITS APPLICATIONS FOR MODELS OF STATISTICAL MECHANICS**\n\n*PIERRE-LOÏC MÉLIOT AND ASHKAN NIKEGHBALI*\n\n49 pp, 20 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** In this paper we complete our understanding of the role played by the lim- iting (or residue) function in the context of mod-Gaussian convergence. The question about the probabilistic interpretation of such functions was initially raised by Marc Yor. After recalling our recent result which interprets the limiting function as a measure of \"breaking of symmetry\" in the Gaussian approximation in the framework of general central limit theorems type results, we introduce the framework of L1-mod-Gaussian convergence in which the residue function is obtained as (up to a normalizing factor) the probability density of some sequences of random variables converging in law after a change of probability measure. In particular we recover some celebrated results due to Ellis and Newman on the convergence in law of dependent random variables arisisng in statistical mechanics.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 1.1 Complex convergence and interpretation of ; 1.2 The method of joint cumulants; 1.3 Basic models; 2 Mod-Gaussian convergence for the Ising model; 3 Mod-Gaussian convergence in L1 and the Curie; 3.1 Ellis-Newman lemma and deconvolution of a ; 3.2 The residue of mod-Gaussian convergence as; 3.3 Application to the Curie-Weiss model; 3.4 Mixed Curie-Weiss-Ising model; 3.5 Sub-critical changes of measures; 3.6 Random walks changed in measure; 4 Local limit theorem and rate of convergence ; 4.1 Speed of convergence; 4.2 Local limit theorem; 5 Mod-Gaussian convergence for the Ising model; 5.1 Joint cumulants of the spins",
      "lang": "en",
